This commit's own message needed --no-verify: the quoted hook runs to
77 characters at its widest, which is exactly the case the 72-to-120
range exists for. Nice to have the demonstration land in the commit
that argues for it, though it does mean the hook rejected the message
that quotes the hook.

An earlier draft of this commit also collapsed the body and notes
limits from soft-72/hard-120 down to a hard 72, reasoning that the
skill and the hook were two sources of truth and the looser one was
winning. Wrong twice over. 35d32b9 introduced 120 deliberately, as
room for code snippets, benchmark output, and long identifiers that do
not wrap; and a hook is a trigger rather than a ban — it stops you and
makes you decide. The two were never in conflict. Recording it because
the wrong story was the more persuasive one.

Whether the hook should itself distinguish the soft limit from the hard
one is a separate question, untouched here. As it stands it fires at
72, so every use of the 72-to-120 range costs a --no-verify.

A follow-up worth considering: have the skill notice a missing
commit-msg hook and offer to install one. Deliberately not done here —
it turns a message-writing skill into a repo-configuring one, and the
argument in this commit is that the width belongs to the repo.

I considered piping notes through `fmt -w 72` rather than grepping
them, and rejected it. fmt would enforce the soft limit as if it were
hard, and on this host it reflows rather than wraps — joining short
lines within a paragraph even under -s, and double-spacing after
sentence ends — so a pasted table or a deliberate break would not
survive. Reporting a violation leaves the judgement where it belongs.

The "no hooks fire on git notes add" claim is tested, not assumed: a
throwaway repo with pre-commit, prepare-commit-msg, commit-msg,
post-commit and post-rewrite all logging showed every one fire on
`git commit` and none on `git notes add`. Only reference-transaction
fires there, and it sees nothing but the ref update.
